1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:03,590
Somewhere in this repository,
we tell clients not

2
00:00:03,590 --> 00:00:05,110
to cache the health response.

3
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:06,600
Where?

4
00:00:06,670 --> 00:00:07,930
And which test checks it?

5
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:09,990
Let's answer that ourselves in Cursor.

6
00:00:10,590 --> 00:00:13,150
We'll jump between a file, its symbols, the

7
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:15,130
code that calls it, and the test that

8
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:16,010
checks the response.

9
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:18,970
The goal isn't a keyboard shortcut speed run,

10
00:00:19,230 --> 00:00:21,210
it's arriving at the right evidence before we

11
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:22,770
ask an agent to change anything.

12
00:00:25,083 --> 00:00:27,203
Start with what you already know.

13
00:00:27,703 --> 00:00:30,243
A command name belongs in the Command Palette.

14
00:00:30,863 --> 00:00:32,863
A file name belongs in Quick Open.

15
00:00:33,683 --> 00:00:36,523
A string belongs in Find or Workspace search.

16
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:41,203
A function belongs in Symbol
Navigation, Definitions, or References.

17
00:00:41,923 --> 00:00:42,943
Those are different questions.

18
00:00:43,763 --> 00:00:45,883
Choosing the right one turns a huge code

19
00:00:45,883 --> 00:00:47,583
base into a short investigation.

20
00:00:48,363 --> 00:00:51,043
We'll use this small working starter to make

21
00:00:51,043 --> 00:00:52,083
each move visible.

22
00:00:54,617 --> 00:00:59,397
Open the Command Palette with
Ctrl-Shift-P on this Linux machine.

23
00:00:59,897 --> 00:01:03,237
Type Keyboard Shortcuts and
read the actual result labels.

24
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:06,617
We want the interactive
settings page, so select

25
00:01:06,617 --> 00:01:09,697
Open Keyboard Shortcuts
without the JSON suffix.

26
00:01:10,777 --> 00:01:12,317
Similar names can open different views.

27
00:01:13,017 --> 00:01:15,417
The palette is a searchable command menu, and

28
00:01:15,417 --> 00:01:18,237
it often shows the assigned
shortcut alongside the command.

29
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:20,637
You don't have to memorize everything today.

30
00:01:23,117 --> 00:01:25,937
Search this settings page
for Go to Definition.

31
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:29,037
The binding is F12 in this installation.

32
00:01:29,957 --> 00:01:31,117
Notice the when column?

33
00:01:31,677 --> 00:01:34,737
The editor needs the appropriate
focus and definition support.

34
00:01:35,417 --> 00:01:37,237
A shortcut is more than a few keys.

35
00:01:37,697 --> 00:01:39,317
It's a command plus a context.

36
00:01:40,097 --> 00:01:42,297
If a shortcut behaves
differently on your machine,

37
00:01:42,737 --> 00:01:45,577
inspect its binding here before
assuming the feature is missing.

38
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:51,923
Close Settings and open
Quick Open with Ctrl-P.

39
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:54,503
Type the path to the handler file, then

40
00:01:54,503 --> 00:01:55,743
choose the matching result.

41
00:01:56,323 --> 00:01:58,903
This searches file names
rather than file contents.

42
00:01:59,483 --> 00:02:02,743
Including the folder helps when
several files share the same name.

43
00:02:03,343 --> 00:02:05,263
Check the breadcrumb after opening it.

44
00:02:05,443 --> 00:02:08,563
We're in the source folder, in handler.ts, in

45
00:02:08,563 --> 00:02:10,383
the fresh clone from the previous lesson.

46
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:15,850
With the editor focused, open find and search

47
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:18,570
for cache control with a
hyphen between the words.

48
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:20,970
Here we have one match in this file.

49
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:23,650
The line sets the response header to no

50
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:25,270
store inside the health route.

51
00:02:25,870 --> 00:02:28,630
Read the surrounding condition
as well as the matching text.

52
00:02:29,270 --> 00:02:31,270
A highlighted string tells us where to look.

53
00:02:31,930 --> 00:02:35,190
Its surrounding control flow
tells us when that line runs.

54
00:02:37,817 --> 00:02:41,417
Now, search the workspace
for create handler, written

55
00:02:41,417 --> 00:02:42,557
as one identifier.

56
00:02:43,917 --> 00:02:46,897
We get five text matches across three files

57
00:02:46,897 --> 00:02:47,677
in this checkout.

58
00:02:48,437 --> 00:02:51,437
There's the definition,
the server that imports and

59
00:02:51,437 --> 00:02:53,797
calls it, and the test file that also

60
00:02:53,797 --> 00:02:54,677
builds a server.

61
00:02:55,357 --> 00:02:57,717
These are text results, grouped by file.

62
00:02:57,937 --> 00:02:59,637
They give us useful leads, but the count

63
00:02:59,637 --> 00:03:03,237
doesn't mean five runtime calls
or five independent tests.

64
00:03:05,683 --> 00:03:10,123
Put src slash double star in the files

65
00:03:10,123 --> 00:03:11,143
to include field.

66
00:03:11,883 --> 00:03:15,343
The results narrow to three
matches in two source files.

67
00:03:16,143 --> 00:03:19,003
The tests disappear because
we excluded their folder

68
00:03:19,003 --> 00:03:21,623
from this search, not
because the tests vanished

69
00:03:21,623 --> 00:03:22,443
from the project.

70
00:03:23,423 --> 00:03:25,803
Clear that filter and the test results return.

71
00:03:26,283 --> 00:03:28,623
Before trusting a missing
result, check the open

72
00:03:28,623 --> 00:03:31,723
folder include and exclude
fields and the search options.

73
00:03:34,017 --> 00:03:36,257
Back in the handler editor, open go to

74
00:03:36,257 --> 00:03:39,017
symbol in editor with control, shift, O.

75
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:41,737
Cursor lists the symbols
recognized in this file,

76
00:03:42,297 --> 00:03:44,917
filter for create handler
and select the function.

77
00:03:45,497 --> 00:03:46,977
We land at its definition.

78
00:03:47,557 --> 00:03:50,597
This uses language support
to navigate code structure.

79
00:03:51,197 --> 00:03:53,337
It isn't a text search, and the at

80
00:03:53,337 --> 00:03:55,397
sign in this picker is not an instruction

81
00:03:55,397 --> 00:03:57,677
to attach code to an agent conversation.

82
00:04:00,183 --> 00:04:02,723
Open the server file and put the caret

83
00:04:02,723 --> 00:04:04,423
on the call to create handler.

84
00:04:04,963 --> 00:04:05,763
Press F12.

85
00:04:06,523 --> 00:04:08,723
Cursor takes us to the function definition in

86
00:04:08,723 --> 00:04:09,543
the handler file.

87
00:04:09,983 --> 00:04:11,523
That's the relationship we wanted.

88
00:04:12,003 --> 00:04:15,163
This server constructs its request
handler from that function.

89
00:04:15,843 --> 00:04:18,383
If navigation can't
resolve a symbol in another

90
00:04:18,383 --> 00:04:21,663
project, check language support
and project configuration.

91
00:04:22,663 --> 00:04:24,803
Guessing from a similar file name is a

92
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:25,583
weaker substitute.

93
00:04:28,283 --> 00:04:32,403
From the definition, open
references with Shift, F12.

94
00:04:33,123 --> 00:04:36,523
This inline view shows five
locations, including the

95
00:04:36,523 --> 00:04:37,923
declaration in this example.

96
00:04:38,763 --> 00:04:41,083
Expand the test file and inspect its use

97
00:04:41,083 --> 00:04:42,083
of the same handler.

98
00:04:42,983 --> 00:04:45,223
References and text search happen to give the

99
00:04:45,223 --> 00:04:46,083
same count here.

100
00:04:46,543 --> 00:04:47,783
They are still different tools.

101
00:04:48,723 --> 00:04:51,143
Language references follow
a recognized symbol.

102
00:04:51,703 --> 00:04:54,503
Text search finds matching characters
within its configured scope.

103
00:04:56,950 --> 00:04:59,450
Open the health test file and find the

104
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:01,050
cache control header assertion.

105
00:05:01,650 --> 00:05:03,950
In the healthy GET case, the test makes

106
00:05:03,950 --> 00:05:06,450
a request and expects the response header to

107
00:05:06,450 --> 00:05:07,530
equal no store.

108
00:05:08,230 --> 00:05:10,890
Now we have a specific
check tied to the behavior.

109
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:14,150
Read the test setup and
the assertion together.

110
00:05:14,870 --> 00:05:17,050
Merely finding the word health in a test

111
00:05:17,050 --> 00:05:19,530
name would not establish
that this header is checked.

112
00:05:22,083 --> 00:05:24,323
The lesson uses Linux shortcuts.

113
00:05:24,883 --> 00:05:27,543
The practice sheet includes
common Windows and Mac

114
00:05:27,543 --> 00:05:30,263
defaults for the same
commands, but your installed

115
00:05:30,263 --> 00:05:31,543
bindings are the authority.

116
00:05:32,703 --> 00:05:36,703
Extensions, remapping, keyboard layout,
and focus can change what a key does.

117
00:05:37,323 --> 00:05:39,463
Use the Command Palette to find the command,

118
00:05:39,703 --> 00:05:41,063
then inspect its binding.

119
00:05:41,723 --> 00:05:44,883
That's a more portable
skill than blindly replacing

120
00:05:44,883 --> 00:05:46,843
every control key with command.

121
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:52,570
Before recording our findings,
check Source Control.

122
00:05:53,190 --> 00:05:54,930
There are no pending file changes.

123
00:05:55,630 --> 00:05:57,310
We navigated and read the code.

124
00:05:57,830 --> 00:05:59,790
We didn't need to rewrite it to understand

125
00:05:59,790 --> 00:06:00,650
these relationships.

126
00:06:01,430 --> 00:06:04,330
This is a useful habit before
handing work to an agent.

127
00:06:04,970 --> 00:06:08,230
Establish the current behavior,
locate the relevant test,

128
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:09,890
and know the starting state.

129
00:06:11,110 --> 00:06:13,970
Navigation evidence helps
define a change, but it doesn't

130
00:06:13,970 --> 00:06:15,610
replace running the relevant checks.

131
00:06:18,183 --> 00:06:22,203
Record four locations, the
header assignment, the handler

132
00:06:22,203 --> 00:06:25,263
definition, the server's
call, and the test assertion.

133
00:06:25,963 --> 00:06:29,123
Include the repository revision
because line numbers can move.

134
00:06:29,803 --> 00:06:32,203
For each location, write what it establishes.

135
00:06:33,083 --> 00:06:35,623
Our map connects a concrete behavior to its

136
00:06:35,623 --> 00:06:38,163
implementation, its wiring, and a check.

137
00:06:38,663 --> 00:06:41,003
That's useful context for a future agent task,

138
00:06:41,363 --> 00:06:43,883
a code review, or another
engineer joining the project.

139
00:06:46,183 --> 00:06:47,203
Quick checkpoint.

140
00:06:47,923 --> 00:06:50,263
You searched only the source folder and found

141
00:06:50,263 --> 00:06:51,283
no matching test.

142
00:06:51,903 --> 00:06:52,683
What's the next move?

143
00:06:53,903 --> 00:06:55,423
Inspect and widen the search scope.

144
00:06:56,003 --> 00:06:58,223
Don't conclude that coverage is missing from a

145
00:06:58,223 --> 00:06:59,003
filtered search.

146
00:06:59,903 --> 00:07:00,703
Second situation.

147
00:07:01,283 --> 00:07:04,103
You know a function's name,
but not its file name.

148
00:07:05,003 --> 00:07:07,983
Workspace text search can
locate candidates, then use

149
00:07:07,983 --> 00:07:11,323
definition and reference
navigation to inspect the actual

150
00:07:11,323 --> 00:07:12,243
symbol relationship.

151
00:07:15,583 --> 00:07:18,923
Your exercise is to trace
the unsupported method

152
00:07:18,923 --> 00:07:20,443
response on the health route.

153
00:07:20,983 --> 00:07:24,323
Locate the condition, find
the response status, and

154
00:07:24,323 --> 00:07:26,223
identify the test that checks it.

155
00:07:27,143 --> 00:07:29,703
Record file paths and line
references at your revision,

156
00:07:30,143 --> 00:07:31,283
plus how you found them.

157
00:07:31,883 --> 00:07:33,163
Keep the checkout unchanged.

158
00:07:33,943 --> 00:07:36,103
If you get stuck, choose the tool based

159
00:07:36,103 --> 00:07:38,943
on what you know—a file name, a string,

160
00:07:39,303 --> 00:07:40,623
a symbol, or a command.

161
00:07:43,183 --> 00:07:45,143
We can now move from a question to

162
00:07:45,143 --> 00:07:47,603
code, follow a symbol to the places that

163
00:07:47,603 --> 00:07:50,103
use it, and locate a relevant assertion.

164
00:07:50,703 --> 00:07:53,783
That's the foundation for working
quickly without losing the plot.

165
00:07:54,283 --> 00:07:56,823
Next, we'll use Cursor Tab for small edits

166
00:07:56,823 --> 00:07:59,403
and learn how to accept
suggestions deliberately.

167
00:08:00,063 --> 00:08:02,623
Before moving on, which
search filter could hide

168
00:08:02,623 --> 00:08:04,403
the very file you are trying to inspect?
